  <!-- ===== OUR APPROACH ===== -->
  <section class="prog-section" id="approach">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow">The SHARP Method</p>
      <h2 class="prog-section__heading">How the SHARP Method works in <span class="nb">O-Level</span> Mathematics</h2>
      <p class="prog-section__sub">Jeremy Lim, a former corporate lawyer (LLB Hons, NUS Faculty of Law), built the SHARP Method from the way lawyers take a problem apart before answering it. Five steps, used on every question.</p>

      <div class="card-grid card-grid--2">
        <div class="prog-card">
          <h3 class="prog-card__title"><span class="step-dot" aria-hidden="true">S</span>See</h3>
          <p class="prog-card__desc">Many students lose marks before they write a line, because they misread the question. At See, your child reads each question the way a lawyer reads a contract. They note the command word (solve, simplify, factorise, prove, show that, sketch), what the marks on offer say about the method, and which topics are being tested. In geometry, that means spotting the angle properties or circle theorems first. In algebra, it means choosing between factorising, the quadratic formula and completing the square.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="prog-card">
          <h3 class="prog-card__title"><span class="step-dot" aria-hidden="true">H</span>Hit</h3>
          <p class="prog-card__desc">Once the question type is clear, your child picks the framework built for it: READ for word problems, LABEL for geometry, PLOT for graphs, DATA for statistics, RULE for trigonometry and SHOW for proofs. Each one follows the mark scheme, so your child knows what each line of working earns. You&rsquo;ll find all six explained below.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="prog-card">
          <h3 class="prog-card__title"><span class="step-dot" aria-hidden="true">A</span>Apply</h3>
          <p class="prog-card__desc">Your child uses the framework on a real past-paper question and writes their own solution. In algebra, that means one operation per line, so the examiner can follow the logic. In geometry, it means a stated reason beside every angle or length. Jeremy watches the screen-shared working and flags missing steps, unclear notation and sign errors as they happen, not a week later. With at most 6 in the group, every student gets feedback on their own working in every lesson.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="prog-card">
          <h3 class="prog-card__title"><span class="step-dot" aria-hidden="true">R</span><span class="step-dot" aria-hidden="true">P</span>Refine &amp; Practise</h3>
          <p class="prog-card__desc">Your child checks every solution against a short checklist for the topic: substitute the answer back, confirm the units match the question, sketch the geometry to see if the answer looks right, and make sure any probability lies between 0 and 1. Then they rework key problems from memory, spaced over weeks rather than crammed, until the framework runs on its own on exam day.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ===== QUESTION-TYPE FRAMEWORKS ===== -->
  <section class="prog-section" id="frameworks">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow">Question-type frameworks</p>
      <h2 class="prog-section__heading">A named framework for every question type on the <span class="nb">O-Level</span> Mathematics paper</h2>
      <p class="prog-section__sub">Your child learns which tool to reach for, and how to use it, for every topic on both papers.</p>

      <div class="card-grid card-grid--2">
        <div class="prog-card">
          <h3 class="prog-card__title">Word problems: READ</h3>
          <p class="prog-card__desc">READ turns a word problem into algebra in four steps. Read the question twice and underline what&rsquo;s being asked, not what&rsquo;s given. Extract the given values and list the unknowns. Assign variables, defining x in words before writing the equation. Derive the equation, solve it, and check the answer makes sense: a speed can&rsquo;t be negative and a number of people must be a whole number. READ stops the most common word-problem mistake: solving the equation correctly but answering the wrong question.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="prog-card">
          <h3 class="prog-card__title">Geometry: LABEL</h3>
          <p class="prog-card__desc">Geometry questions reward the reasoning as well as the calculation, so every value needs a stated reason. LABEL (Label, Apply, Build, Execute, Link) sets the order of work. Label the diagram with every known measurement, equal side, parallel line and right angle. Apply the relevant properties: angle sum of a triangle, angles on a straight line, alternate angles, circle theorems, similarity ratios. Build the solution step by step, with the reason beside every value. Execute each calculation cleanly. Link back to check the answer fits the original conditions. Method marks go to correctly stated reasons, and LABEL makes writing them a habit.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="prog-card">
          <h3 class="prog-card__title">Graphs and functions: PLOT</h3>
          <p class="prog-card__desc">Graph questions test whether your child understands the function behind the curve. PLOT (Parameters, Locate, Obtain, Trace) covers them in four steps. Parameters: name the function type (linear, quadratic, cubic or exponential); the sign of the leading coefficient gives the shape. Locate the key features before plotting: intercepts, turning point, line of symmetry, asymptotes. Obtain a table of values that includes the turning point and intercepts. Trace a smooth curve, label both axes with a clear scale, and mark the key features. Most students who lose marks here can plot the points; what they miss are the features the examiner wants.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="prog-card">
          <h3 class="prog-card__title">Statistics: DATA</h3>
          <p class="prog-card__desc">Statistics rewards tidy organisation, not mental arithmetic. DATA (Define, Arrange, Tabulate, Answer) lays out the working that earns the marks. Define: name the measure the question wants (mean, median, mode, standard deviation, interquartile range or probability). Arrange: put the data in a frequency table, ordered list or cumulative frequency table. Tabulate: show the working in between, such as &Sigma;fx for the mean, cumulative frequencies for the median, (x &minus; x&#x0304;)&sup2; for standard deviation. Answer: state the result in context, with units and sensible rounding. Correct tabulation earns method marks even when the final answer has an arithmetic slip.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <details class="prog-more">
        <summary class="btn btn--sm prog-more__toggle"><span class="prog-more__show">Show all 6 frameworks</span><span class="prog-more__hide">Hide the other 2 frameworks</span></summary>
        <div class="card-grid card-grid--2">

        <div class="prog-card">
          <h3 class="prog-card__title">Trigonometry: RULE</h3>
          <p class="prog-card__desc">Trigonometry is where students most often pick the wrong method, such as SOH-CAH-TOA on a triangle with no right angle. RULE (Right-angle check, Use the correct formula, Label, Execute) settles the choice. Right-angle check: if there&rsquo;s a right angle, use SOH-CAH-TOA. If not, two sides and the included angle means the cosine rule; a side and its opposite angle means the sine rule. Use the correct formula. Label sides and angles by the standard convention (lowercase sides, uppercase opposite angles). Execute, then sense-check: the angle should be acute or obtuse as expected, a bearing is measured clockwise from north, and the length has to fit the triangle.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="prog-card">
          <h3 class="prog-card__title">Proofs and &ldquo;show that&rdquo;: SHOW</h3>
          <p class="prog-card__desc">&ldquo;Show that&rdquo; and proof questions are where many students give up. SHOW (State, Hunt, Order, Write) gives them a structure. State the result needed at the top of the working. Hunt for the theorems, identities or algebraic properties that get there. Order the chain from the given information to the result before writing. Write each step with the reason or theorem that justifies it. The mark scheme wants reasoning at every step, and SHOW builds it in.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      </details>
    </div>
  </section>

